    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d150">

                <pb facs="0330" n="298" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v10/pg298"/>

                <head>150. Telegram From Central Intelligence Agency Personnel in Nicaragua to the
                    Central Intelligence Agency<anchor n="0" xml:id="d150-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Puerto Cabezas,
                            Nicaragua</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1961-04-19T12:07:00-06:00">April 19, 1961, 12:07
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>902. 1. Situation for air support beachhead completely out of our hands. This
                    morningʼs effort extended us to the limit. Have now lost 5 Cuban pilots, 6
                    co-pilots, 2 American pilots, and one co-pilot, and observer either killed or
                    captured. Unknown number aircraft out of commission. (Will advise.)</p>
                <pb facs="0331" n="299" xml:id="pg299"/>
                <p>2. Two crews shot down today were shot down during period “positive aggressive”
                    Navy air support and cover granted and for one hour 1130Z to 1230Z 19 April. Per
                    Hqs. 4834<anchor n="1" xml:id="d150-f2"/> (OUT 7237).</p>
                <p>3. Still have faith. Awaiting your guidance.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d150-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d150-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Central Intelligence Agency, DDO/LA/COG Files: Job 82-00679R, Box 3, Gen
                    Maxwell Taylor, Green Study Group, Vol. II. Top Secret; Flash.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d150-f2" n="1" xml:id="d150-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 4834 to the Air Commander at Puerto Cabezas, April 19, 3:04 a.m.,
                    informed him that the Navy would provide 1 hour of air cover over the beaches at
                    the Bay of Pigs, and enjoined him to make the best possible use of the
                    opportunity to attack Cuban forces on the approaches to the airfield. (Ibid.)
                    For text, see the Supplement.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
